I used Google Gemini for this lab so I could be helped in understanding how to compare character arrays and structure my selection statements, as well as to organize my README documentation. It helped me understand why standard string comparisons wouldn't work for the color mixer program and how to check multiple conditions cleanly. When writing `color_mixer.cpp`, I asked how to compare the user's input to `"red"`, `"blue"`, and `"yellow"`, and it explained that I needed to use `strcmp(first_color, "red") == 0` instead of `==` because `==` only compares memory addresses for `char` arrays rather than the actual text inside them. I accepted this suggestion and also decided to validate that both inputs were valid primary colors first before checking any color combinations. I verified the code by testing reversed inputs like `blue` and `red` as well as invalid inputs like `orange` and `blue` in OnlineGDB, and the console accurately printed `purple` for the valid pair and the error message for the invalid color. I also checked `bmi.cpp` with `150` pounds and `70` inches, confirming the formula printed `21.52 — Optimal weight` and properly included the `18.5` and `25.0` boundaries. One thing I learned from this assignment is how to safely read and compare C-style `char` arrays using `setw` and `strcmp`. One thing I still need to practice is writing long compound `if / else if` conditions with `&&` and `||` without missing parentheses.
